The easiest way to test this is to create a custom search client like this:

```
import { SearchClient } from 'instantsearch.js';

export const searchClient: SearchClient = {
   search(requests) {
      return fetch("http://localhost:3000/api/search", {
         method: "post",
         headers: {
            "Content-Type": "application/json",
         },
         body: JSON.stringify({ requests }),
      })
         .then((res) => res.json())
         .catch((err) => {
            console.error(err);
         });
   },
};
```

In /api/search I throw an error to simulate a failed search. If catch is not added to waitForResults you will get the infinite page loading. If you handle the error (by adding a catch to waitForResults) and log it, you will see the same maximum call stack problem.
